    (v_topic_id, 'A 6-year-old child refuses to bear weight on the right leg. The hip is held in flexion, abduction, and external rotation. Temperature is 39.0°C. Laboratory evaluation shows a WBC count of 15,000/mm³ and an ESR of 50 mm/hr. According to the Kocher criteria, what is the probability that this child has septic arthritis rather than transient synovitis?', '["Exactly 0%", "About 25%", "> 90% (Very high probability)", "< 10% (Very low probability)", "About 50%"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Kocher criteria differentiate septic arthritis from transient synovitis. The 4 criteria are: non-weight-bearing, fever > 38.5°C, ESR > 40 mm/hr, and WBC > 12,000/mm³. Having all 4 predictors yields a 99% probability of septic arthritis; 3 predictors yield a 93% probability. This child has 4 predictors.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 13-year-old female gymnast complains of insidious onset, mechanical low back pain that is exacerbated by extension activities (e.g., back walkovers). Neurologic exam is normal. Oblique radiographs of the lumbar spine demonstrate a ''collar'' on the ''Scotty dog''. What is the specific anatomic defect in this condition?', '["Congenital failure of segmentation of the lumbar vertebrae", "Herniation of the nucleus pulposus", "Defect in the vertebral body endplate (Schmorl node)", "Fracture of the transverse process", "Defect in the pars interarticularis"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Spondylolysis is a stress fracture or defect of the pars interarticularis. It is highly prevalent in adolescent athletes participating in sports involving repetitive lumbar hyperextension. On oblique radiographs, a radiolucent gap in the pars resembles a collar on the neck of the ''''Scotty dog''''.'),
