    (v_topic_id, 'A 4-month-old male infant is diagnosed with Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip (DDH) via ultrasound. The hip is reducible. What is the standard first-line treatment for this patient?', '["Open reduction and pelvic osteotomy", "Pavlik harness", "Triple diapering", "Observation only", "Closed reduction and spica casting"]'::jsonb, 1, 'For infants < 6 months of age with a reducible dysplastic or dislocated hip, the Pavlik harness is the gold standard treatment. It maintains the hip in flexion and abduction, promoting concentric reduction and acetabular remodeling. Closed reduction is typically reserved for failures or children 6-18 months.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 3-week-old infant, born breech, presents for a routine well-child check. Examination reveals asymmetric thigh skin folds and a positive Ortolani maneuver on the left. What is the most appropriate initial imaging modality to confirm the diagnosis?', '["MRI of the hips", "Bone scan", "Ultrasound of the hips", "CT scan of the pelvis", "AP radiograph of the pelvis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip (DDH) is most accurately assessed with ultrasound in infants under 4-6 months of age because the femoral head and acetabulum are predominantly cartilaginous and not fully visible on radiographs. A positive Ortolani test (reduction of a dislocated hip) strongly suggests DDH.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #47060: A 13-year-old boy presents with frequent ankle sprains and a painful, rigid flatfoot. Examination reveals severe restriction of subtalar motion and spasm of the peroneal muscles. Radiographs show a ''C-sign'' on the lateral view. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Flexible pes planus", "Posterior tibial tendon insufficiency", "Calcaneonavicular coalition", "Sever disease", "Talocalcaneal coalition"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Detailed orthopedic assessment of the pediatric patient is required to identify these specific conditions correctly and avoid serious long-term complications.'),
