    (v_topic_id, 'A 5-year-old falls from a trampoline and sustains a significantly displaced extension-type supracondylar humerus fracture. Upon examination, the child is unable to make an ''OK'' sign (unable to flex the IP joint of the thumb and DIP joint of the index finger). Which nerve is most likely injured?', '["Ulnar nerve", "Anterior interosseous nerve (AIN)", "Axillary nerve", "Radial nerve", "Musculocutaneous nerve"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The anterior interosseous nerve (AIN), a branch of the median nerve, is the most commonly injured nerve in extension-type supracondylar humerus fractures. Injury manifests as an inability to flex the IP joint of the thumb (flexor pollicis longus) and the DIP of the index finger (flexor digitorum profundus), resulting in an inability to make the ''''A-OK'''' sign.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 14-day-old infant is brought to the clinic for unilateral right clubfoot (talipes equinovarus). The parents ask about the treatment plan. According to the Ponseti method, the deformities are corrected with serial casting in a specific sequence. Which component of the deformity is corrected LAST?', '["Equinus", "Varus", "Cavus", "Adductus", "Supination"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In the Ponseti method for clubfoot, the CAVE deformities are corrected sequentially: Cavus (elevating the first ray), Adductus, Varus, and finally Equinus. The equinus correction often requires a percutaneous Achilles tenotomy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 2-year-old child refuses to bear weight on the right leg. The hip is held in flexion, abduction, and external rotation. Temperature is 39.0°C. Laboratory evaluation shows a WBC count of 15,000/mm³ and an ESR of 50 mm/hr. According to the Kocher criteria, what is the probability that this child has septic arthritis rather than transient synovitis?', '["Exactly 0%", "About 50%", "< 10% (Very low probability)", "> 90% (Very high probability)", "About 25%"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Kocher criteria differentiate septic arthritis from transient synovitis. The 4 criteria are: non-weight-bearing, fever > 38.5°C, ESR > 40 mm/hr, and WBC > 12,000/mm³. Having all 4 predictors yields a 99% probability of septic arthritis; 3 predictors yield a 93% probability. This child has 4 predictors.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #84548: A 4-year-old child presents with a limp that started yesterday morning. Two weeks ago, he had a mild URI. He is well-appearing. He refuses to bear weight fully but has only mild pain with passive hip ROM. Labs show a normal WBC count and an ESR of 15 mm/hr. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Slipped capital femoral epiphysis", "Legg-Calv\u00e9-Perthes disease", "Juvenile idiopathic arthritis", "Transient synovitis", "Septic arthritis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Detailed orthopedic assessment of the pediatric patient is required to identify these specific conditions correctly and avoid serious long-term complications.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #64926: A 2-year-old child, who is at the 95th percentile for weight, presents with progressively worsening bowing of the left leg (genu varum). Radiographs show a prominent ''beak'' at the medial metaphysis of the proximal tibia. What is the diagnosis?', '["Physiologic genu varum", "Rickets", "Osgood-Schlatter disease", "Achondroplasia", "Infantile Blount disease"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Detailed orthopedic assessment of the pediatric patient is required to identify these specific conditions correctly and avoid serious long-term complications.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 5-year-old child refuses to bear weight on the right leg. The hip is held in flexion, abduction, and external rotation. Temperature is 40.0°C. Laboratory evaluation shows a WBC count of 15,000/mm³ and an ESR of 50 mm/hr. According to the Kocher criteria, what is the probability that this child has septic arthritis rather than transient synovitis?', '["Exactly 0%", "About 25%", "< 10% (Very low probability)", "About 50%", "> 90% (Very high probability)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The Kocher criteria differentiate septic arthritis from transient synovitis. The 4 criteria are: non-weight-bearing, fever > 38.5°C, ESR > 40 mm/hr, and WBC > 12,000/mm³. Having all 4 predictors yields a 99% probability of septic arthritis; 3 predictors yield a 93% probability. This child has 4 predictors.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 1-week-old infant, born breech, presents for a routine well-child check. Examination reveals asymmetric thigh skin folds and a positive Ortolani maneuver on the bilateral. What is the most appropriate initial imaging modality to confirm the diagnosis?', '["Bone scan", "CT scan of the pelvis", "AP radiograph of the pelvis", "MRI of the hips", "Ultrasound of the hips"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip (DDH) is most accurately assessed with ultrasound in infants under 4-6 months of age because the femoral head and acetabulum are predominantly cartilaginous and not fully visible on radiographs. A positive Ortolani test (reduction of a dislocated hip) strongly suggests DDH.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #45398: A 7-month-old infant is referred to orthopedics for asymmetric gluteal folds and a leg length discrepancy. Which imaging modality is considered the gold standard to evaluate for Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip (DDH) at this specific age?', '["CT scan", "AP radiograph of the pelvis", "MRI of the pelvis", "Bone scan", "Ultrasound of the hips"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Detailed orthopedic assessment of the pediatric patient is required to identify these specific conditions correctly and avoid serious long-term complications.');
